          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong Green-Schwarz mechanism
            (named after
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Green"} Michael Green
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Schwarz"} John Schwarz
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            involving higher
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
            of the original action functional disappear.
          %p More in detail:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                An
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral quantization
                is said to be
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} anomalous
                if it is only locally identified with a function on the configuration space of fields, but is globally instead a section of a line
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
                (usually equipped with
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            %li
              %p
                Given two anomalous action functionals in this sense, it may happen that while the two corresponding line bundles on configuration space are each nontrivial, their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
                becomes trivializable. In this case one can consider the non-anomalous action function given by the sum of the two anomalous action functionals. This is what is called
                %strong anomaly cancellation
                of one piece of an action functional against another.
            %li
              %p The two main sources of examples for action functionals that are anomalous are
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    the standard
                    %span.newWikiWord
                      fermionic action functional
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/new/fermionic+action+functional"} ?
                    (see there for details) for chiral fermions
                  %ul
                    %li
                      this is a section of the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pfaffian+line+bundle"} Pfaffian line bundle
                      for the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
                %li
                  %p
                    the action functional for
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cocycles
                    (higher connection, higher
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+charge"} electric
                    and
                    = succeed "s:" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
                  %ul
                    %li
                      this is a
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
                      of the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression"} transgression
                      of the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
                      arising as the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cup+product"} cup product
                      of the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+charge"} electric
                      and
                      = succeed "." do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic differential cocycles
          %p
            A
            %strong Green–Schwarz mechanism
            is the addition of an action functional for higher differential cocycles with
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
            cancels a given
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pfaffian+line+bundle"} Pfaffian line bundle
          %p
            In the more strict and original sense of the word,
            %em the
            Green–Schwarz mechanism is the application of this procedure in the theory called
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression"} transgression
            of a degree-12 class in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            that factorizes as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi I
                      %mn 8
                    %mo ∧
                    %msub
                      %mi I
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_8 \wedge I_4
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} strings
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi J
                    %mi electric
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi J
                    %mi magnetic
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J_{electric} \wedge J_{magnetic}
            that the anomaly for the corresponding higher gauge theory in the presence of magnetic charges gives rise to. So the original Green–Schwarz anomaly cancellation mechanism consist of modifying the “naive” action functional for heterotic supergravity by adding the contribution that corresponds to adding a magnetic current of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi I
                    %mn 4
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  j_B \,\coloneqq\, I_4
                  \\,.
          %h2#the_higher_magnetic_charge_anomaly The higher magnetic charge anomaly
          %h3#YMactionfunctional The higher abelian Yang-Mills action functional…
          %p
            Consider on some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mi diff
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\nabla] \in H_{diff}^{n+1}(X)
            modeled in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            in degree
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundle+with+connection"} circle n-bundle with connection
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                in degree
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=1
                this is an
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
            %li
              %p
                in degree
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=2
                this is a
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
            %li
              %p
                in degree
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 3
                a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
          %p
            Canonically associated to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            is its
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi cl
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F_\nabla \in \Omega^{n+1}_{cl}(X)
                  \\,.
          %p
            The abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+action+functional"} Yang-Mills action functional
            for our gauge field (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of higher order
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi YM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mi diff
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp\big(
                  \\mathrm{i} S_{YM}(-)
                  \\big)
                  \\;\colon\;
                  H_{diff}^{n+1}(X) \to \mathbb{C}
          %p
            that sends the field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi YM
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mo ∇
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo ⋆
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mo ∇
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp\big( \mathrm{i} S_{YM}(-)\big)
                  \\;\colon\;
                  [\nabla]
                  \\mapsto
                  \\exp\big(
                  \- \mathrm{i}
                  \\textstyle{\int}_X F_\nabla \wedge \star F_\nabla
                  \\big)
          %p
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential"} exponential
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} differential form
            obtained as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wedge+product"} wedge product
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature+form"} curvature form
            with its image under the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star+operator"} Hodge star operator
            correspondonding to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+manifold"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            The fact that this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/map"} map
            defined in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            is a well defined function on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            means that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge invariant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi g
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\nabla \stackrel{g}{\to} \nabla'
          %p
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            is invariant (due to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-group"} higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            being
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+infinity-group"} abelian
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mo ∇
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∇
                        %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\nabla = F_{\nabla'}
          %h3#WithElectricCharge … with electric charge …
          %p
            The
            %a{:href => "#YMactionfunctional"} above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            describes the dynamics of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            all by itself, with no interactions with other fields or with
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+particle"} fundamental particles
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} fundamental branes
          %p
            A distribution of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+charge"} electric charge
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is modeled itself by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"} cocycle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat j_E
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
            in degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi dim
                  %mi X
                  %mo −
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} dim X - n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msubsup
                    %mi H
                    %mi diff
                    %mrow
                      %mi dim
                      %mi X
                      %mo −
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [\hat j_E]
                  \\;\in\;
                  H_{diff}^{dim X - n}(X)
                  \\,,
          %p
            so that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j_E
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat j_E
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+current"} electric current
            form. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            that encodes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+force"} Lorentz force
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            exerted on this electric charge distribution is locally on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+charts"} coordinate charts
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊂
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset X
            given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi U
                    %mo ∧
                    %msub
                      %mi j
                      %mi E
